      * *note FilterCondition: MonodromySolverOptions, => ..., default value
        null,
      * *note GraphInitFunction: MonodromySolverOptions, => ..., default value
        completeGraphInit,
      * new tracking routine
      * *note NumberOfEdges: MonodromySolverOptions, => ..., default value
        null,
      * *note NumberOfNodes: MonodromySolverOptions, => ..., default value
        null,
      * *note NumberOfRepeats: MonodromySolverOptions, => ..., default value
        10,
      * PointArrayTol (missing documentation) => ..., default value .0001, 
      * *note Potential: MonodromySolverOptions, => ..., default value null, 
      * *note Randomizer: MonodromySolverOptions, => ..., default value null, 
      * *note SelectEdgeAndDirection: MonodromySolverOptions, => ..., default
        value selectFirstEdgeAndDirection,
      * *note StoppingCriterion: MonodromySolverOptions, => ..., default value
        null,
      * *note TargetSolutionCount: MonodromySolverOptions, => ..., default
        value null,
      * *note Verbose: MonodromySolverOptions, => ..., default value false, 
  * Outputs:
      * p, an instance of the type *note Point: (NAGtypes)AbstractPoint,,
        parameter values. If not part of the input, they are chosen uniformly
        as complex numbers w/ modulus 1.
      * sols, an instance of the type *note PointArray: PointArray,, containing
        solutions to PS specialized at p.

Description
===========

The output of *note monodromySolve: monodromySolve, is opaque. This method is
intended for users uninterested in the underlying *note HomotopyGraph:
HomotopyGraph, and its satellite data. When the number of solutions is small,
it can help to increase options like *note NumberOfNodes:
MonodromySolverOptions, or *note NumberOfEdges: MonodromySolverOptions, above
their default value, as shown here:

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : setRandomSeed 0                                                         |
| -- setting random seed to 0                                                 |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = 0                                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : R = CC[a,b,c,d,e,f][x,y];                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : q  = a*x^2+b*y+c;                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : l = d*x+e*y+f;                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : (sys, sols) = solveFamily(polySystem{q,l}, NumberOfNodes=>3)            |
|                                                                             |
|o5 = (sys, {{1.7698-.292574*ii, 2.03236+1.25703*ii}, PointArray( 2 points: 1 |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     0 ), {-.770087+.316588*ii, .35355-.743892*ii}})                         |
|                                                                             |
|o5 : Sequence                                                                |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

This function is somewhat less flexible than *note monodromySolve:
monodromySolve,.



